// Reference for Configs/brand.xcconfig — the real, GITIGNORED brand file.
//
// Copy this file to Configs/brand.xcconfig and replace every value below with the
// real identity for the brand being built. The values here are PLACEHOLDERS: they
// build, but they name nothing that exists — no provisioning profile, no associated
// domain, no App Group. Brand identity is deliberately kept out of version control,
// so this template is the only thing the repository knows about the key set.
//
// A missing Configs/brand.xcconfig is a hard build error at config parse:
//     Configs/base.debug.xcconfig:7: error: could not find included file 'brand.xcconfig' in search paths
//
// An INCOMPLETE Configs/brand.xcconfig is not caught at build time. A key present
// here but absent there expands to an empty string in Info.plist, and AppConfig.Brand
// traps at launch naming the key. After adding a key here, tell the team to add it.
//
// URLs are stored as HOST (and path) ONLY, never a scheme. An xcconfig treats "//"
// as a comment start anywhere on the line, so "https://example.com" would silently
// resolve to "https:". Swift composes the scheme, matching
// ProductUniversalLink.scheme = "https".
//
// Config-axis values (compiler flags, ICON_SUFFIX, the .develop suffix) live in
// Configs/base.*.xcconfig, not here. Every key below must be present and non-empty.
//
// BRAND_DEEPLINK is the scheme base. Each configuration appends its own suffix to it
// (Configs/base.*.xcconfig) so every installable app id owns a distinct scheme. The base
// must not collide with any other installed brand's: iOS resolves a scheme collision
// non-deterministically.

BRAND_DISPLAY_NAME = Example
BRAND_BUNDLE_ROOT = com.example.app
BRAND_DEEPLINK = exampleapp
BRAND_SHARE_ROOT = example.com
BRAND_SHARE_ROOT_TEST = test.example.com
BRAND_CASH_SYMBOL = CASH
BRAND_FIAT_SYMBOL = $
BRAND_TERMS_HOST = example.com/terms
BRAND_PRIVACY_HOST = example.com/privacy
BRAND_CONTACT_EMAIL = info@example.com
